	SECTION 4
Using Results Responsibly
Author Check is designed to help you decide where to look, not what to conclude. This section explains how to weigh what you find when screening authors, peer reviewers and editors.
FOUR PRINCIPLES
* An indicator is a prompt, not a finding. None of the indicators can determine misconduct by themself.
* Read indicators together. One highlight tells you where to look. Several highlights that point the same way deserve more attention than one on its own.
* Look behind the number. Open the flagged publications to see what type of flag it is, when it happened and why.
* Absence of a flag is not clearance. The retraction process is slow, and many problematic papers have not been reported.
SCREENING BY ROLE
If you are checking…
	Look at first
	Keep in mind
	An author
	Flagged publications (type, date and reasons), then self-citations and the percentile of publication output.
	A high output percentile may reflect real productivity. A missing self-citation result for an early career researcher may not be meaningful.
	A peer reviewer
	Flagged publications, then co-authors with network flags and co-authors with flagged publications.
	Network indicators describe the researcher’s co-author network. They say nothing directly about the quality of their research or reviews.
	An editor
	Flagged publications, then the co-author indicators.
	Treat any highlight as a prompt to look closer and to follow your organization’s own process.
	WHAT THE INDICATORS CANNOT TELL YOU
* Intent. A retraction can follow an honest mistake that the researcher reported themself. Some retractions are due to publication procedures or third-party rights issues with an image.
* What has not been reported. There may be far more researchers with research integrity issues than we know.
* Anything outside the data. Indicators are calculated from the publications Dimensions has assigned to researcher profiles, and from the sources described in the Technical Guide.
* Researchers outside the analysis. Self-citation analysis covers only recently active researchers with at least 5 eligible publications.
SECTION 5
Working with the Data
This section covers moving around the dashboard, working with tables and charts, drilling down, and exporting.
NAVIGATING THE DASHBOARD
* Selecting reports. Click the report or submenu item you want (it will turn blue) to update the dashboard.
* Active report. Only one report can be active at a time. Others close automatically once a submenu item is selected.
USING TABLES AND CHARTS
To…
	Do this
	Sort a table
	Use the arrows next to the column name.
	Re-order columns
	Drag and drop the column.
	See what a tile shows
	Hover over the icons on the tile for a description of the data.
	Learn what a field or filter means
	Scroll to the About this data section at the end of each page.
	DRILL-DOWNS AND LINKS
* Accessing drill-downs. Where available, click a tile, bar or point on a visualization to see the drill-down information.
* Drill-down display. You will either see a table with the drill-down information appear immediately or be given one or more links to explore more data.
* Links to Dimensions. Some elements link to the main Dimensions web app. When you click one, a pop-up appears with the link to the relevant Dimensions page.


NOTE: DRILL-DOWN LIMIT
Drill-downs are limited to a maximum of 500 results.
